<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hyōgo Prefecture</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hyōgo Prefecture is a prefecture of the Kansai region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, reaching from the Inland Sea to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobe">Kobe</a>. It was formed in the 1870s from parts of the old provinces of Settsu, Harima, Tajima, Tamba and Awaji. It contains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himeji-castle">Himeji Castle</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> since 1993, and was struck by the Great Hanshin earthquake in 1995.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hyogo-prefecture.jpg" alt="Hyōgo Prefecture" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Laitr Keiows · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Night_view_of_Rokk%C5%8D_Island_and_Higashinada-ku,_Kobe,_Japan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Hyōgo covers about 8,400 square kilometres and borders Kyoto Prefecture to the east, Osaka Prefecture to the south-east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tottori">Tottori</a> prefectures to the west. It has coasts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-japan">Sea of Japan</a> to the north and the Seto Inland Sea to the south, and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awaji-island">Awaji Island</a> it also meets the Pacific. The north is sparsely populated, apart from Toyooka, and the central highlands hold only tiny villages. Most people live on the southern coast, part of the Osaka–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobe">Kobe</a>–Kyoto metropolitan area. The north gets abundant snow in winter.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The prefecture includes the former provinces of Harima, Tajima and Awaji and parts of Tanba and Settsu. In 1180, near the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heian-period">Heian period</a>, Emperor Antoku, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-no-kiyomori">Taira no Kiyomori</a> and the imperial court moved briefly to Fukuhara, in what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobe">Kobe</a>, and the capital stayed there for five months. In 1995 the Great Hanshin earthquake, of magnitude 6.9, devastated southern Hyōgo, destroying large parts of Kobe and Awaji as well as Nishinomiya and Ashiya and killing nearly 6,500 people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobe">Kobe</a> is the capital and largest city, and other major cities are Himeji, Nishinomiya and Amagasaki. Kobe Port is one of the largest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and since 1975 prefectural law has kept it free of nuclear weapons. Hyōgo belongs to the Hanshin Industrial Region and holds two Riken research institutes, at Kobe and Harima, as well as the SPring-8 synchrotron at Harima. Its cultural sites include several National Treasures, the preservation districts of Izushi and Sasayama, the hot springs of Arima and the Takarazuka Revue. In 1963 Hyōgo became the first Japanese prefecture to enter a sister state relationship with an American state, Washington.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hyōgo Prefecture” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Himeji Castle in <b>Hyōgo Prefecture</b> was first fortified in 1333 and rebuilt on its present scale by Ikeda Terumasa from 1601 to 1609.</blockquote><a href="/work/himeji-castle">Himeji Castle · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>It became part of <b>Hyogo Prefecture</b> in 1871, and the castle became a World Heritage Site in 1993.</blockquote><a href="/state/himeji-domain">Himeji Domain</a></li></ul></section>
